```json
{
  "root_causes": [
    {
      "service": "user",
      "fault_kind": "pod_failure",
      "evidence": [
        {"kind": "metric", "sql": "SELECT attr_k8s_pod_name pod, metric, count(*) n, min(value) mn, max(value) mx FROM abnormal_metrics WHERE attr_k8s_pod_name LIKE 'user-%' AND metric IN ('k8s.container.restarts','k8s.container.ready') GROUP BY ALL", "claim": "The user pod's container restarts climb to 2-6 and k8s.container.ready stays 0 during the incident; normal period shows 0 restarts."},
        {"kind": "trace", "sql": "SELECT service_name, span_name, attr_span_kind, count(*) n FROM abnormal_traces WHERE service_name='user' GROUP BY ALL UNION ALL SELECT service_name, span_name, attr_span_kind, count(*) FROM normal_traces WHERE service_name='user' GROUP BY ALL", "claim": "The user service has 1 server span in the incident versus 290 in the normal period."},
        {"kind": "trace", "sql": "SELECT span_name, attr_status_code, count(*) n, round(avg(duration)/1e6,3) avg_ms FROM abnormal_traces WHERE service_name='frontend' AND span_name='user.User/CheckUser' AND attr_span_kind='Client' GROUP BY ALL", "claim": "Frontend's user.User/CheckUser client calls return Error in about 0.02 ms average, 355 of 356, versus Unset normally."}
      ]
    }
  ],
  "propagation": [
    {"from": "user", "to": "frontend", "evidence": [
      {"kind": "trace", "sql": "SELECT f.span_name, f.attr_status_code, c.service_name cs, c.attr_status_code cst, count(*) n FROM abnormal_traces f LEFT JOIN abnormal_traces c ON c.trace_id=f.trace_id AND c.parent_span_id=f.span_id WHERE f.service_name='frontend' AND f.attr_span_kind='Server' AND c.service_name IN ('user','attractions') GROUP BY ALL", "claim": "Frontend HTTP /cinema, /museums, /restaurants, /reservation and /user return Error after failing CheckUser, never reaching attractions."}
    ]}
  ]
}
```

The only fault I'm naming is the crash-looping `user` pod (`pod_failure`), which reached users through `frontend`. The `attractions` drop is a knock-on effect of the failed user check, not a separate fault. The `attractions` pod's CPU does rise from about 0.002 to 3.6 cores, but its own span durations stay normal (0.72 ms against 0.62 ms) and nothing user-facing shows that CPU effect. I left it out as an unconfirmed side symptom; it could be a second fault if the evaluator counts it.
